    "text": "<p>Lyophilic colloids are stable due to charge and solvation, they can be used to protect Lyophobic colloids</p><p>Emulsions are liquid-liquid type of colloids</p><p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>FeCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is added to the excess of hot water, a positively charged sol of hydrated ferric oxide is formed due to adsorption of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> ions. However, when ferric chloride is added to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi></math> solution, a negatively charged sol is obtained with adsorption of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;ions.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Fe</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#183;</mo><msub><mi>xH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math><br />Positively charged</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Fe</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math><br />Negatively charged</p>",
